{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Tested the sharpest remaining worry — that a vote response from a removed member could be counted and let a candidate reach a quorum of the new configuration without a majority of its voters. The mechanism is real (removal leaves the tally untouched, candidates do install config-event handlers, and the counter filters only observers), but unreachable: campaigning is refused while committed entries are unapplied, applying a change requires commitment, and a candidate learns of new commits only from a leader, which demotes it and clears its votes. Recorded the negative with that premise, plus bounds: an out-of-band config event or a forged response would reopen it. No execution requested.\"}"}}
